<CaseStudy
  title="The Radar"
  tagline="A pipeline that turns public LinkedIn chatter and EU official sources into structured, verifiable regulatory intelligence."
  description="Case study: a pipeline that turns public LinkedIn chatter and EU official sources into structured, verifiable regulatory intelligence."
  current="home"
>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Product footprinting in Europe is being rewritten by a wave of regulation, ESPR,
    PPWR, EUDR, CSRD, the Empowering Consumers directive, and the wave doesn't
    announce itself in one place. Vendors make claims on LinkedIn that may or may not
    survive scrutiny. Official acts appear in the Official Journal with no alerting.
    Competitors signal strategy in posts nobody has time to read. Staying current meant
    reading everything, by hand, forever, so I built a machine that reads it for me.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    Five times a day, unattended, the Radar fetches new public LinkedIn posts from a
    curated watchlist plus keyword searches, checks EU official sources for acts
    touching the regulations I track, classifies every item against my open research
    questions, verifies vendor claims against their sources, clusters what people are
    worried about into canonical themes, and renders it all into dashboards I can
    triage in minutes. Alerts land in my notes inbox, routed by tags. Steady state:
    ~26 posts a day, ~$13 a month.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/radar/radar-stack.svg" alt="The Radar pipeline: LinkedIn posts via a third-party API and EU official sources feed a verbatim, immutable raw archive; one LLM pass per post classifies against research questions and verifies claims, with failures degrading instead of aborting; annotations are stored as regenerable artifacts; theme clustering maps 827 unique phrasings to about 65 canonical labels; outputs are insights dashboards, a tag-routed notes inbox, and a research-question kanban; the human opens proposed questions, curates sources, and decides." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The pipeline, end to end. Blue is where the model runs, grey is deterministic
      code, orange is me. The model never writes to the archive, and the human only
      ever does the judgment calls.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="discipline" data-toc="The discipline">The discipline: raw is sacred, derived is disposable</h2>
  <p>
    The same rule as everywhere else I build: <strong>AI drafts, rules decide, humans
    approve</strong>, and here it has a storage-layer twin. The raw archive is
    verbatim, immutable, and written first; everything the LLM produces lands as
    a <em>separate, regenerable annotation artifact</em> alongside it. Delete the
    annotations and you lose nothing that can't be recomputed; corrupt them and the
    raw truth is untouched.
  </p>
  <p>
    Failure handling is contractual, not hopeful. A scraping outage logs a SKIP and
    exits cleanly instead of spamming tracebacks; an unparseable LLM reply degrades
    that one post's classification visibly instead of killing the run; a short-lived
    OAuth token triggers one refresh-and-retry, never a mid-flight death. Official
    sources get a special rule: they always classify and alert but are
    <em>never verified</em>, checking a primary source against official sources is
    circular, so the verdict honestly stays null.
  </p>

  <h2 id="themes" data-toc="827 phrasings, 65 labels">827 phrasings, 65 labels: the clustering problem</h2>
  <p>
    The dashboards track "industry themes", but LLM-extracted concerns are unique
    phrasings, so across 511 posts there were <strong>748 distinct ways of saying
    things</strong>, and the recurrence signal (≥2 posts per theme) mathematically
    couldn't fire. The top-3 "themes" were the same theme in different words.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix is LLM clustering with the model on a leash: phrasings are clustered into
    canonical labels in batches, but <strong>coverage is enforced in code</strong>,
    only submitted phrasings may appear in the output, first-cluster-wins, labels
    capped at six words. One 798-phrasing call timed out at 180 seconds, so the work
    is batched and the artifact rewritten after each batch, an interrupted refresh
    resumes by resubmitting only the tail. Result: <strong>827 phrasings mapped into
    ~65 canonical labels</strong>, deep links from the themes table match every member
    phrasing, and the raw annotations stay immutable, an absent mapping simply
    degrades to raw phrasings.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/radar/dashboard-themes.jpg" alt="The industry-themes table on the live dashboard: canonical theme labels with last-7-days vs prior-7-days counts and trend arrows, supply chain disruption and volatility, consumer purchasing power pressure, cross-border sourcing barriers, EU compliance burden and fatigue, food system environmental pressure, fragmented EPR rules across Member States." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The clustered themes table on the live dashboard: canonical labels, 7-day deltas,
      and deep links into the filtered triage view. Recurrence signals that literally
      could not exist on raw phrasings.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="cost" data-toc="Cost engineering">Cost engineering: measured, then cut 94%</h2>
  <p>
    The scraping API is pay-per-result, and dedup was local, so every run re-paid for
    the latest 20 posts of every source. I found out the hard way: the $5 free tier
    evaporated in one day and the pipeline died on a 403. Projection at that cadence:
    ~$115/month.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two measured fixes. First, every run now passes a <code>scrapeUntil</code> cutoff
    derived from the archive's newest post, never re-pay for what's already stored;
    the watchlist run went from <strong>$0.52 to $0.038</strong>. Second, the searches
    ran 5×/day but yielded only 2–5 genuinely new posts per query per day, so they
    moved to the 06:00 slot only, full coverage, ≤24h latency on non-watchlist
    voices. Steady state: <strong>~$13/month</strong>, and the 403 now degrades
    cleanly with the API's own message surfaced.
  </p>

  <h2 id="official" data-toc="Official sources">Official sources: three doors, all probed live first</h2>
  <p>
    LinkedIn chatter is half the picture; the other half is law. EUR-Lex proper
    bot-blocks automation, but the CELLAR SPARQL endpoint answers anonymously, so
    new Official Journal acts arrive via one grouped query per run (the naive
    <code>DISTINCT</code> shape multiplied rows 4× and a LIMIT would have silently
    dropped the oldest days, the grouped shape doesn't). EFRAG has no RSS at all, so
    a small HTML parser state machine reads its teaser markup (never regex). The
    Commission's press corner serves an Angular shell, but its backing JSON API
    answers anonymously. Each adapter got a live probe <em>before</em> any pipeline
    code, sits behind the same port, and fails soft to empty, an official-source
    outage never blocks the paid scrape.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/radar/dashboard-timeline.jpg" alt="The regulatory timeline on the live dashboard: one swimlane per regulation, CSRD/ESRS, ESPR and Digital Product Passport, Empowering Consumers, PEF, Construction Products, EUDR, Packaging, with milestone dots on a shared date axis, official-source links, and per-lane chatter counts." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The regulatory timeline on the live dashboard: one lane per regulation, milestone
