      client like this?" view.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="loop" data-toc="The knowledge loop">The knowledge loop: questions, not just posts</h2>
  <p>
    The Radar is organized around <strong>research questions</strong>, not keywords,
    a kanban of open questions that every classification is tagged against. After each
    run, a discovery pass scans recent annotations for recurring themes and files
    candidates as <em>proposed</em> questions, recurrence threshold, window, and cap
    all enforced in code, so the model can propose but never promote. Opening a
    question stays a human move. Closing one <strong>harvests an evidence pack</strong>
   , lifetime post count, verdict spread, corroborated highlights with citations,
    straight into my notes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The delivery work itself mirrors onto a Jira/Linear bake-off as a product-owner
    hierarchy, local files stay the source of truth, the mirror never writes back,
    and API calls happen only on drift. And anonymized client archetypes power a
    per-client obligations view: <em>what does this regulation concretely demand of a
    client like this, and when?</em>, the question the whole machine exists to answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-it-stands">Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    Live and unattended: five scheduled runs a day, ~26 posts a day classified, 350+
    tests green, ops reviews on a cadence with decisions recorded. The honest limits:
    it's a single-operator system, the watchlist and the regulatory curation are the
    ongoing human work, and the verdict layer is only as good as the sources posts
    cite, vendor claims default to <em>unverified</em> until evidence says otherwise.
    That skepticism is a feature: in this domain, an unverified claim is exactly the
    thing worth knowing about.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>
